{"instructions": "Explain how a university registration system should handle two students simultaneously requesting the last seat. Discuss transactions, duplicate prevention, and the benefits and risks of caching. This is a theoretical answer, not a coding task.", "criterion": "Cache latency versus staleness trade-off", "rubric": {"policy": "Assess theoretical understanding. A valid conceptual explanation suffices; do not require SQL code, every listed alternative, or topics outside the selected criterion. Separate independent criteria. An explicitly false claim is incorrect, not just missing coverage.", "criterion": "Cache latency versus staleness trade-off", "professional_principle": "Caching can reduce read latency but cached seat availability can be stale. Cached availability may be a display hint; authoritative enrollment decisions must be validated against database state, with capacity concurrency handled by the separate reservation criterion.", "valid_solutions": ["Explain faster cached reads and stale-data risk, with a final database validation instead of trusting cached availability alone.", "Equivalent separation of cached display/read optimization and authoritative database enrollment decisions, with appropriate invalidation or freshness handling."], "scope": "A policy accepting enrollment solely because the cache reports a free seat is explicitly wrong, not merely an omitted discussion. A final database check resolves the cache-trust issue but does not automatically satisfy atomicity or safe concurrent capacity reservation."}, "submission": "Author: SyntheticAuthorCedar\nI use one database transaction to create the enrollment and consume a seat, committing both together. If either fails, rollback undoes both changes. A UNIQUE constraint on (student, course) prevents the same student enrolling twice, but does not reserve capacity for different students. For capacity I perform a conditional update that reduces remaining seats only when the count is positive. I accept the enrollment only if that update succeeds and roll back the transaction if enrollment creation fails. Thus competing requests cannot both consume the same final seat. Cached availability speeds up display reads but may be stale, so cached results never authorize enrollment; the conditional database reservation is authoritative."}
